### Part A: Importing and Inspecting a CSV
A1 import employees.csv into a data frame named emp_df using read.csv() with stringAsFactors = FALSE.
```{r}
emp_df <- read.csv("employees.csv", stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
```
A2 Display the structure, the first 5 rows, and summary of emp_df.
```{r}
str(emp_df)
head(emp_df, 5)
summary(emp_df)
```
A3 Print the number of rows and the number of columns of emp_df, and the class of every column (hint: sapply (em_df, class)).
```{r}
nrow(emp_df)
ncol(emp_df)
sapply(emp_df, class)
```
A4 Print the mean salary, the maimum age, and the number of employees in each department using table().
```{r}
mean(emp_df$salary)
max(emp_df$age)
table(emp_df$department)
```

### Part B: Creating Data Frames
B1 Create a data frame new_hires with the same column names as emp_df and these three employees:
```{r}
new_hires <- data.frame(
  emp_id = c(21, 22, 23),
  name = c("Emp21", "Emp22", "Emp23"),
  department = c("IT", "HR", "Sales"),
  sex = c("F", "M", "F"),
  age = c(28, 32, 27),
  salary = c(60000, 52000, 55000),
  years_service = c(1, 3, 2),
  rating = c(4, 3, 4),
  stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)
```
B2 Use rbind() to append new_hires to emp_df, saving the result as emp_all. Print the dimensions of emp_all.
```{r}
emp_all <- rbind(emp_df, new_hires)
dim(emp_all)
```
B3 Add a column bonus to all emp_all equal 5% of salary, rounded to the nearest whole number.
```{r}
emp_all$bonus <- round(emp_all$salary * 0.05)
```

